   "text": {
    "bs4_get_text": "\n\nApproval limits (2016) - NBC Intranet\n\n NBC Intranet » Purchasing\n\nPurchasing home\nHow to raise an order\nError messages\nApproval limits\nSupplier list\nContacts\nForms\nLedgerline 7 training\nApproval limits (2016)\nRoleLimit\nPurchase executive£150,000\nSenior buyer£300,000\nPurchase manager£600,000\nPlant head£2,000,000\n\nLimits are on the net value before VAT.\nApproval limits (2016)\nAll purchase orders, stock requisitions, and capital commitments raised across the Manchester factory and the Leeds unit must comply with the company authorisation tiers. Staff are reminded that all limits apply strictly to the net value before VAT. Value added tax must not be included when determining which band of authority an order falls under, but the tax portion must still be entered correctly for accounting purposes.\nOrders must not be artificially split into multiple smaller orders to circumvent approval thresholds. System audits run regularly from the finance office, and any attempt to bypass controls by raising parallel requisitions against the same supplier account will be flagged. Where an ongoing service or regular intake of cycle components requires multiple deliveries, the total anticipated commitment over the entire order period governs the level of authorisation needed.\nDepartment managers hold delegated authority up to their established tier for operational overheads and regular production inventory. Requisitions that exceed standard department limits require counter-signing by a director. Any non-budgeted capital expenditure, building works, or tooling investments require the formal endorsement of the managing director before commitments are placed with suppliers.\nWhen processing through Ledgerline ERP, users must select the designated authoriser from the standard workflow list. An order remains at an unapproved status and cannot be printed, transmitted, or converted into an active commitment until the approving manager enters their sign-off. If an authoriser is absent from the business, their designated deputy may act only where formal standing arrangements exist within the system administration settings. Emergency verbal approvals are not recognised by the accounts department, and invoices received without a corresponding approved order will be held in query pending retrospective review.\nLast modified 03/11/2016 by DRAMSDEN. Exported from the old intranet (NBC-INTRANET01) in 2019 before it was switched off.",
    "trafilatura": "Approval limits (2016)\n| Role | Limit |\n|---|---|\n| Purchase executive | £150,000 |\n| Senior buyer | £300,000 |\n| Purchase manager | £600,000 |\n| Plant head | £2,000,000 |\nLimits are on the net value before VAT.\nApproval limits (2016)\nAll purchase orders, stock requisitions, and capital commitments raised across the Manchester factory and the Leeds unit must comply with the company authorisation tiers. Staff are reminded that all limits apply strictly to the net value before VAT. Value added tax must not be included when determining which band of authority an order falls under, but the tax portion must still be entered correctly for accounting purposes.\nOrders must not be artificially split into multiple smaller orders to circumvent approval thresholds. System audits run regularly from the finance office, and any attempt to bypass controls by raising parallel requisitions against the same supplier account will be flagged. Where an ongoing service or regular intake of cycle components requires multiple deliveries, the total anticipated commitment over the entire order period governs the level of authorisation needed.\nDepartment managers hold delegated authority up to their established tier for operational overheads and regular production inventory. Requisitions that exceed standard department limits require counter-signing by a director. Any non-budgeted capital expenditure, building works, or tooling investments require the formal endorsement of the managing director before commitments are placed with suppliers.\nWhen processing through Ledgerline ERP, users must select the designated authoriser from the standard workflow list. An order remains at an unapproved status and cannot be printed, transmitted, or converted into an active commitment until the approving manager enters their sign-off. If an authoriser is absent from the business, their designated deputy may act only where formal standing arrangements exist within the system administration settings. Emergency verbal approvals are not recognised by the accounts department, and invoices received without a corresponding approved order will be held in query pending retrospective review.",
    "structured": "Approval limits (2016)\n Role | Limit\n Purchase executive | £150,000\n Senior buyer | £300,000\n Purchase manager | £600,000\n Plant head | £2,000,000\n Limits are on the net value before VAT.\nApproval limits (2016)\n All purchase orders, stock requisitions, and capital commitments raised across the Manchester factory and the Leeds unit must comply with the company authorisation tiers. Staff are reminded that all limits apply strictly to the net value before VAT. Value added tax must not be included when determining which band of authority an order falls under, but the tax portion must still be entered correctly for accounting purposes.\n Orders must not be artificially split into multiple smaller orders to circumvent approval thresholds. System audits run regularly from the finance office, and any attempt to bypass controls by raising parallel requisitions against the same supplier account will be flagged. Where an ongoing service or regular intake of cycle components requires multiple deliveries, the total anticipated commitment over the entire order period governs the level of authorisation needed.\n Department managers hold delegated authority up to their established tier for operational overheads and regular production inventory. Requisitions that exceed standard department limits require counter-signing by a director. Any non-budgeted capital expenditure, building works, or tooling investments require the formal endorsement of the managing director before commitments are placed with suppliers.\n When processing through Ledgerline ERP, users must select the designated authoriser from the standard workflow list. An order remains at an unapproved status and cannot be printed, transmitted, or converted into an active commitment until the approving manager enters their sign-off. If an authoriser is absent from the business, their designated deputy may act only where formal standing arrangements exist within the system administration settings. Emergency verbal approvals are not recognised by the accounts department, and invoices received without a corresponding approved order will be held in query pending retrospective review.\n Last modified 03/11/2016 by DRAMSDEN. Exported from the old intranet (NBC-INTRANET01) in 2019 before it was switched off."
   }
  },
